
########################################################################
# PRAVAAH · M4 — Cross-Border Money Movement (UPI ↔ PayNow · ledger · idempotency)
#
# VS Code "REST Client" / JetBrains HTTP client.  Base = /api/v1/...
#
# M3 made a person's reputation trustworthy across borders. M4 is what ACTS on
# that trust: it moves money.
#
# ─── READ THIS BEFORE SENDING ANYTHING ────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. EVERY MUTATION NEEDS AN `Idempotency-Key`.
#    Not optional, and the 400 you get without one is the point: a client that
#    forgets it would have its retry treated as a NEW payment. Change the key
#    when you mean a new payment; REUSE it to prove a retry replays.
#
# 2. AMOUNTS ARE INTEGER MINOR-UNIT STRINGS.
#    `"10000"` is ₹100.00 or S$100.00 — paise and cents, never rupees. Sent as
#    strings because `JSON.parse` turns any numeric literal into a float before
#    a line of our code runs, and there is no way to detect afterwards whether
#    the value we hold is the value that was sent.
#
# 3. EVERY TRANSFER NEEDS A QUOTE FIRST.
#    Including domestic ones, where there is nothing to convert. A quote is a
#    PRICED INTENT: the exact numbers the user was shown, frozen server-side,
#    single-use, 90 seconds. The transfer reads them back rather than
#    recomputing — otherwise the app shows one total and the server derives
#    another, and the user is charged a figure they never agreed to.
#
# 4. THE PAYEE NAME MUST BE CONFIRMED.
#    `confirmedRegisteredName` has to match what the payee's BANK holds. That
#    is an NPCI mandate and the single most effective control against
#    social-engineering fraud: the attacker controls the address string but not
#    what the bank calls the account behind it. §21 shows the mismatch → 409.
#
# ─── PREREQUISITES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   • A VERIFIED user (M0). `npm run prisma:seed`, then complete KYC.
#   • A UPI ID (§02) — needed to receive, to raise a collect, and to mint a QR.
#   • Wallet balance. There is no cheat endpoint: run a topup (§05) and approve
#     it on the mock rail (§06). That IS the real inbound path.
#
# ─── THE MONEY-SAFETY SHOWPIECE ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   §80 forces the rail's "late SUCCESS after we timed out" case: the transfer
#   shows FAILED, reconciliation detects the rail actually settled, and it
#   self-corrects to SUCCESS — booking the loss as a RECEIVABLE rather than
#   breaking the trial balance. §90 shows the trial balance netting to zero.
########################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000/api/v1
@token = PASTE_A_USER_ACCESS_TOKEN
@adminToken = PASTE_AN_ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN

# Filled in as you go.
@beneficiaryId = PASTE_FROM_§10
@quoteId = PASTE_FROM_§20
@transferId = PASTE_FROM_§21
@collectId = PASTE_FROM_§05
@mandateId = PASTE_FROM_§50
@qrRef = PASTE_FROM_§40

########################################################################
# 01 · THE WALLET
########################################################################

### Balances, UPI IDs and remaining limits — the whole wallet screen.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/wallet
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The caps, and how much of each is left.
# Note the ASYMMETRY: ₹1,00,000/day domestic vs SGD 1,000/day cross-border.
# Roughly a factor of sixty. Any code that treated the two rails alike would
# be wrong by that much — which is why `corridor` is a stored column.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/limits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The account statement — LEDGER ENTRIES, not transactions.
# A transaction list says what the user tried to do; this says what happened to
# their money, with the reserve, the fee and the refund as separate lines. When
# somebody disputes a balance, this is the document that answers them.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/wallet/statement?limit=50
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

########################################################################
# 02 · YOUR UPI ID
########################################################################

### Allocate one. Idempotent — an existing active handle comes back as-is.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/upi-handles
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Or ask for a specific one. Reserved names (`support`, `refund`, `admin`…)
# are refused: somebody holding `support@pravaah` could impersonate us to every
# other user, and the fraud would be indistinguishable from an official request.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/upi-handles
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "vpa": "ravi.test" }

### List them.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/upi-handles
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

########################################################################
# 05 · ADD MONEY (a collect against your OWN account)
########################################################################

### First, add your own bank account as a payee. `isSelf` is VERIFIED against
# M0's penny-drop, never believed — it exempts the payee from the
# new-payee cooling period, so a client-set flag would bypass it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "handleType": "ACCOUNT_IFSC",
  "accountNumber": "123456789012",
  "ifsc": "HDFC0001234",
  "displayName": "My HDFC account",
  "isSelf": true
}

### Then top up. Returns a collect the payer (you) must approve in their bank app.
# We never see a UPI PIN — authorisation happens in the bank/NPCI secure component.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/topup
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: topup-{{$guid}}

{
  "sourceBeneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "amountMinor": "5000000",
  "currency": "INR"
}

########################################################################
# 06 · THE MOCK RAIL — playing the payer's PSP app
#
# Development only; refused when NODE_ENV=production, because these endpoints
# carry exactly the authority a real rail has — they can approve a debit
# against somebody's account.
########################################################################

### What is waiting for this payer, as their own app would show it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/pending/123456789012%7CHDFC0001234

### Approve it. Routed through the SAME method a signed webhook reaches, so the
# credit, the ledger posting and the state machine behave exactly as in production.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/collect/{{collectId}}/respond
Content-Type: application/json

{ "approve": true }

### Or decline — `ZA`, the real NPCI code for "declined by the customer".
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/collect/{{collectId}}/respond
Content-Type: application/json

{ "approve": false, "reason": "Not expecting this" }

########################################################################
# 10 · PAYEES — and the name check that actually stops fraud
########################################################################

### Resolve a UPI ID to the name its BANK holds. No money moves.
# The whole reason this is a separate call: it has to happen BEFORE the decision
# it informs. Folded into the transfer, the name would arrive after the user had
# already committed.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries/resolve
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "vpa": "priya.sharma@ybl" }

### Add a domestic payee.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "handleType": "UPI_VPA",
  "vpa": "priya.sharma@ybl",
  "displayName": "Priya"
}

### A CROSS-BORDER payee. Still a UPI ID — that is the point of the linkage:
# the sender enters the recipient's registered UPI ID, not a PayNow proxy.
# This one is additionally screened against the compliance watchlists, and
# cannot be paid until that screening has actually completed (fail-closed).
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "handleType": "PAYNOW_UPI",
  "vpa": "amma.kumar@oksbi",
  "displayName": "Amma",
  "country": "IN"
}

### An address that does not exist → recorded INVALID with a reason, not an error.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "handleType": "UPI_VPA",
  "vpa": "invalid.person@ybl",
  "displayName": "Nobody"
}

### A sanctions hit → BLOCKED, and NOT unblockable by the user.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "handleType": "PAYNOW_UPI",
  "vpa": "sanctioned.payee@oksbi",
  "displayName": "Test screening",
  "country": "IN"
}

### List them. Response carries `coolingPeriod` for a newly added payee, so the
# app can explain the ₹5,000 / 24-hour cap up front instead of refusing at the
# confirmation screen.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Block one — distinct from removing, which could be undone in seconds with a
# fresh cooling period.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/beneficiaries/{{beneficiaryId}}/block
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "They scammed me" }

########################################################################
# 20 · PRICING — the locked, single-use quote
########################################################################

### Quote a DOMESTIC transfer. Rate exactly 1:1, no fee (NPCI prohibits an MDR
# on UPI P2P/P2M, so a fee here would be non-compliance, not pricing).
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceCurrency": "INR",
  "destCurrency": "INR",
  "mode": "SOURCE",
  "amountMinor": "250000",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER"
}

### Quote a CROSS-BORDER remittance from the SEND side.
# Response shows BOTH rates — mid and customer — so the spread is visible rather
# than implied. A remittance product that shows only its own rate is asking to be
# trusted about the difference.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceCurrency": "SGD",
  "destCurrency": "INR",
  "mode": "SOURCE",
  "amountMinor": "10000",
  "type": "REMITTANCE",
  "purposeCode": "P1301"
}

### The same corridor from the RECEIVE side: "they must get exactly ₹20,000".
# Rounds the SOURCE amount UP, because rounding it down would deliver a paisa
# short of the figure the user named — and one paisa short on a school fee is a
# rejected payment.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceCurrency": "SGD",
  "destCurrency": "INR",
  "mode": "DEST",
  "amountMinor": "2000000",
  "type": "REMITTANCE",
  "purposeCode": "P1301"
}

### 400 — a domestic transfer carrying an RBI cross-border purpose code.
# Filing a FEMA purpose against a rupee-to-rupee payment misreports it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "sourceCurrency": "INR",
  "destCurrency": "INR",
  "mode": "SOURCE",
  "amountMinor": "250000",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P1301"
}

########################################################################
# 21 · SENDING MONEY
########################################################################

### Send. No amount in the body — that lives on the quote. Three confirmations
# instead, each closing a specific failure mode.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: transfer-{{$guid}}

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "narration": "Rent share",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "250000",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

### RETRY THE EXACT REQUEST ABOVE with the SAME Idempotency-Key.
# Returns the SAME transaction id. No second debit. This is the whole point.

### 409 — the same key with a DIFFERENT body.
# Not a retry: a client bug or an attack. Replaying the first response would
# silently discard whatever this request actually asked for.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: REUSE_THE_KEY_FROM_ABOVE

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "narration": "DIFFERENT NOTE",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "250000",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

### 409 — the confirmed payee name does not match the record.
# The NPCI mandate, enforced rather than displayed.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: transfer-{{$guid}}

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "250000",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "SOMEBODY ELSE",
  "confirmedName": true
}

### 409 — the total the user saw disagrees with the quote (a stale screen).
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: transfer-{{$guid}}

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "1",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

### 400 — no Idempotency-Key.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "type": "P2P",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "250000",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

### 403 — over the corridor cap. Try SGD 2,000 cross-border (cap is SGD 1,000/day).
# Quote it first, then send: the rejection names the cap and what remains.

### Status. The client polls US, never the rail — a client asking the rail
# directly would bypass the reconciliation that decides what an answer MEANS.
# `isFinal` tells it when to stop polling.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers/{{transferId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The receipt: the user's own ledger movements plus any refunds.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers/{{transferId}}/receipt
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### History.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers?limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Retry a failed one. Mints a NEW transaction against a FRESH quote — re-driving
# the failed row would give one id two rail references and two ledger histories.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers/{{transferId}}/retry
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: retry-{{$guid}}

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "250000",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

########################################################################
# 30 · REQUESTING MONEY (collect)
#
# Domestic only. UPI↔PayNow is payer-initiated, so "request money from abroad"
# is a screen that can never succeed — refused with that reason.
########################################################################

### Ask somebody to pay you.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/collect
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: collect-{{$guid}}

{
  "payerHandle": "priya.sharma@ybl",
  "amountMinor": "150000",
  "currency": "INR",
  "purposeCode": "P2P_TRANSFER",
  "narration": "Dinner split",
  "expiresInSeconds": 300
}

### Requests you sent.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/collect?side=RAISED
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Requests for YOU to pay. A separate query, never merged: "you owe ₹500" and
# "you are owed ₹500" are opposite facts.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/collect?side=RECEIVED
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Cancel one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/collect/{{collectId}}/revoke
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

########################################################################
# 40 · QR CODES
########################################################################

### A STATIC QR — amount-less, reusable, the shopfront sticker. Not a payment
# instruction (the payer types the amount), so it needs no signature.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "STATIC",
  "currency": "INR",
  "purposeCode": "MERCHANT_PAYMENT",
  "mcc": "5812",
  "narration": "Chai stall"
}

### A DYNAMIC QR — amount-bearing, so it IS a payment instruction: signed,
# short-lived, and consumed on first success. Unsigned, it could be edited in
# transit (`am=1.00` → `am=100.00`) with the real VPA beside it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "DYNAMIC",
  "amountMinor": "12050",
  "currency": "INR",
  "purposeCode": "MERCHANT_PAYMENT",
  "mcc": "5812",
  "expiresInSeconds": 900
}

### Scan. Parsed SERVER-SIDE — a QR is attacker-supplied input that resolves to a
# payee, and two parsers that disagree are two answers to "who gets the money".
# Returns both the PRINTED name (advisory) and the BANK-REGISTERED name (the control).
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr/scan
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "payload": "upi://pay?pa=ravi.test%40pravaah&pn=Chai%20Stall&am=120.50&cu=INR" }

### REJECTED — a duplicated `pa` parameter. A real attack: implementations
# disagree about which value wins, so a preview screen can render one payee
# while the payment uses the other. Nothing is picked.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr/scan
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "payload": "upi://pay?pa=shop%40pravaah&pa=attacker%40evil&cu=INR" }

### REJECTED — more decimal places than the currency has. `am=100.999` for rupees
# is malformed or crafted; rounding it would resolve an ambiguity about somebody's
# money by guessing.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr/scan
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "payload": "upi://pay?pa=shop%40pravaah&am=100.999&cu=INR" }

### Pay a scanned QR. The amount still comes from a quote: a scanned code
# PROPOSES an amount, it does not set the price.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/qr/pay
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: qrpay-{{$guid}}

{
  "payload": "upi://pay?pa=priya.sharma%40ybl&pn=Priya&am=120.50&cu=INR",
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedTotalDebitMinor": "12050",
  "confirmedRegisteredName": "PRIYA SHARMA",
  "confirmedName": true
}

########################################################################
# 50 · UPI AUTOPAY (mandates)
########################################################################

### Set up a mandate. Returns PENDING — the payer must approve it in their own
# app with their UPI PIN. A mandate we marked ACTIVE on creation would be a
# standing debit permission the user never authorised.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/mandates
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: mandate-{{$guid}}

{
  "beneficiaryId": "{{beneficiaryId}}",
  "frequency": "MONTHLY",
  "amountRule": "EXACT",
  "maxAmountMinor": "500000",
  "currency": "INR",
  "purposeCode": "LOAN_REPAYMENT",
  "narration": "EMI",
  "validFrom": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",
  "validUntil": "2027-08-01T00:00:00Z",
  "confirmedMandate": true
}

### Approve it on the mock rail — the UPI AutoPay authorisation screen.
# This is where the UMN (UPI Mandate Number) is assigned, which is why the create
# response does not carry one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/mandate/PASTE_mandateRef
Content-Type: application/json

{ "approve": true }

### The mandate, with its debit history — including SKIPPED and FAILED rows.
# A user asking "why wasn't I charged?" needs to see that the debit was skipped
# because their balance was short.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/mandates/{{mandateId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Present a debit. Must be ≥24h ahead so the pre-debit notice can be sent —
# NPCI requires it, and the scheduler REFUSES to execute a debit without one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/mandates/{{mandateId}}/debits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: debit-{{$guid}}

{
  "amountMinor": "500000",
  "dueAt": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z",
  "narration": "September EMI"
}

### 400 — a debit larger than the authorised ceiling. Re-checked at debit time,
# not only at creation: the merchant chooses the amount later.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/mandates/{{mandateId}}/debits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: debit-{{$guid}}

{ "amountMinor": "99900000", "dueAt": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z" }

### Pause — reversible. Revoke — permanent.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/mandates/{{mandateId}}/action
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "action": "PAUSE", "reason": "Tight month" }

########################################################################
# 60 · DISPUTES (UDIR)
########################################################################

### Raise one against a payment. The SLA deadline is stamped server-side and
# returned — an obligation the customer can hold us to, not a support promise.
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers/{{transferId}}/dispute
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "NOT_CREDITED",
  "description": "I was debited but my landlord says nothing arrived in his account."
}

### 409 — the same complaint retyped. One open dispute per (payment, kind).
POST {{baseUrl}}/payments/transfers/{{transferId}}/dispute
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "kind": "NOT_CREDITED",
  "description": "Still nothing has arrived, please look into this again."
}

### Track them.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/disputes
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

########################################################################
# 80 · THE MONEY-SAFETY SHOWPIECE — a late settlement, corrected
#
# The single most expensive event in payments: we time out, mark a transfer
# FAILED, and the rail settles it anyway. The sender has their money back, the
# beneficiary has the payment, and nobody funded it.
#
# HOW TO STAGE IT
#   1. Set PAYMENTS_SIM_FORCE_OUTCOME=late-success and restart. §90 documents
#      every scripted outcome.
#   2. Send a transfer (§21). It goes PENDING.
#   3. Wait for the first status check (90s) — or force one via §90's recheck.
#      The rail reports FAILED with `U54` (a timeout code), so we do the honest
#      thing and mark it FAILED. The reserve goes back to the sender.
#   4. Wait past the simulator's settle delay, then recheck again.
#      Reconciliation now sees SUCCESS, and self-corrects — booking the payout as
#      a RECOVERY against a RECEIVABLE, because the money genuinely left AND the
#      sender was already refunded. Both are true and neither can be undone.
#   5. §90's trial balance still nets to ZERO. That is the whole point: the loss
#      is BOOKED and visible, not hidden.
########################################################################

### Force the rail's answer directly, without waiting.
# Goes through the REAL webhook path with a REAL signature, so signature
# verification and replay dedupe both apply — a demo that bypassed them would be
# demonstrating a code path that does not exist in production.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/fire-webhook
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "railRef": "PASTE_railRef_FROM_THE_ADMIN_DETAIL_VIEW",
  "status": "SUCCESS",
  "railCode": "00",
  "kind": "payment"
}

### Send it TWICE with the same body. The second is deduped on
# `(provider, eventId)` — a rail that retries its callback must not settle twice.

### Every scripted outcome, and what each proves.
GET {{baseUrl}}/mock/upi/script


########################################################################
# 90 · ADMIN — oversight and the break-glass controls
########################################################################

### THE TRIAL BALANCE. Zero in every currency, always.
# Since every posting set is validated as balanced before it is written, a
# non-zero result cannot come from a bad posting — it means entries reached the
# table WITHOUT passing through the ledger service. That is the one number to
# page somebody about.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/trial-balance?detailed=true
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Operations overview: what is stuck, what is disputed, what is in suspense.
# The suspense figure is read from the LEDGER, not summed from transactions — the
# ledger is what we are actually holding.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/stats
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Payments waiting on a HUMAN — DEEMED and RISK_HELD. The only states where
# money is parked pending a decision nobody has made yet.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions?needsAttention=true
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### One payment, everything about it: the ledger legs, the rail events, the
# reconciliation trail, the reversals.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions/{{transferId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Ask the rail again, now.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions/{{transferId}}/recheck
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Force a reconciliation sweep. Reports how many late settlements it corrected.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/reconcile
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### The reconciliation trail — append-only, one row per check.
# "PENDING at 10:00, PENDING at 10:02, SUCCESS at 10:05 after we had given up" is
# the entire story of a late-settlement incident; an upsert would leave one frame.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/reconciliations?status=MISMATCH
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Resolve a DEEMED transaction by hand. ADMIN only.
# The two options are opposites and both irreversible: SETTLE credits the
# beneficiary, REVERSE gives the money back. Guessing wrong pays twice or not at
# all — which is exactly why the saga parks these instead of choosing, and why
# this demands an evidence reference rather than only a reason.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions/{{transferId}}/resolve-deemed
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resolution": "SETTLE",
  "evidenceRef": "HDFC statement line 2026-07-27 #4412",
  "reason": "The bank statement confirms the credit reached the beneficiary.",
  "confirmed": true
}

### Force a refund. Routed through the normal refund path, so the rail leg goes
# first and the remaining-refundable bound applies.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions/{{transferId}}/refund
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amountMinor": "250000",
  "reason": "Dispute upheld — the beneficiary confirmed non-receipt in writing.",
  "confirmed": true
}

### Release a risk hold. Re-enters the saga at VALIDATED, not RESERVED: a hold can
# sit for hours, and the balance or the day's limit may have moved.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/transactions/{{transferId}}/risk
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "RELEASE",
  "reason": "Customer confirmed the payee by phone; device re-verified."
}

### The dispute work queue, across every user. SLA-soonest first.
# Deliberately a superset of sla-breaches below: a queue that showed only what is already late
# would let every dispute go late before anyone could act on it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/disputes?openOnly=true&orderBy=sla&limit=50
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### The same queue narrowed to the failures, for a lead who wants only those.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/disputes?breachedOnly=true
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Fraud claims specifically — UNAUTHORISED is a statement about the ACCOUNT, not the payment.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/disputes?kind=UNAUTHORISED&orderBy=created
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Disputes past their SLA — the queue an operations lead watches.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/disputes/sla-breaches
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### The raw ledger, filterable. Every entry, append-only, per leg.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/ledger?accountType=SUSPENSE&limit=100
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
